      "text": "To solve the problem, we first need to understand the behavior of the floor function applied to the square roots of integers. For each integer \\( n \\) from 1 to 16, \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor\\) will be the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(\\sqrt{n}\\).\n\nWe can break this down step-by-step:\n\n1. Calculate \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor\\), \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor\\), \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor\\), \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor\\), and so on up to \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor\\).\n\n2. Sum these values.\n\nLet's compute this using Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the range of integers\nimport math\n\n# Initialize the sum\ntotal_sum = 0\n\n# Iterate over the range 1 to 16\nfor n in range(1, 17):\n    total_sum += math.floor(math.sqrt(n))\n\nprint(total_sum)\n```\n```output\n38\n```\nThe final answer to the problem is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{38}\n\\]",
